notes.content:
{key}AORTIC STENOSIS (AS):{/key}
• Failure of aortic valve to open adequately during {hl}LEFT VENTRICULAR SYSTOLE{/hl}
• Creates obstruction to {hl}LEFT VENTRICULAR OUTFLOW{/hl}

{key}LOCATIONS OF OBSTRUCTION:{/key}
• {hl}VALVULAR:{/hl} At valve level (most common)
• {hl}SUPRAVALVULAR:{/hl} Above valve
• {hl}SUBVALVULAR:{/hl} Below valve (from muscular hypertrophy)

{key}Most common cause:{/key} Calcific degeneration of a {hl}BICUSPID{/hl} valve

{key}NORMAL AORTIC VALVE:{/key}
• Three leaflets
• Orifice: {ok}3-4 cm²{/ok}
• Opens during systole → Blood ejected to aorta
• Closes during diastole → Prevents backflow

{key}IN AORTIC STENOSIS:{/key}
• Obstruction to LV outflow
• Sustained pressure gradient across valve
• Left ventricular {hl}PRESSURE OVERLOAD{/hl}
• {hl}Concentric LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY{/hl}

{key}CONSEQUENCES OF HYPERTROPHY:{/key}
• Relative myocardial ischemia
• Angina pectoris (even without CAD)
• Arrhythmias
• Left ventricular failure

{key}PROGRESSION:{/key}
• Early: Compensated (normal cardiac output maintained)
• Late: LV dilates + Wall thins → Systolic dysfunction
• During exercise: Cardiac output cannot increase adequately
• → Syncope, Arrhythmias, Sudden death

{warn}CRITICAL POINT:{/warn} When valve area ↓ to {hlpu}1/3 of normal{/hlpu}, symptoms appear; Without surgery, death occurs in {hlpu}2-3 years{/hlpu}

{key}HISTORY:{/key}
• Murmur since childhood → Congenital
• History of rheumatic fever → Rheumatic

{key}ASYMPTOMATIC (Mild-Moderate):{/key}
• For years, may be discovered incidentally

{key}CLASSIC TRIAD (Severe AS) - D-A-S:{/key}

1. {hl}EXERTIONAL DYSPNEA:{/hl}
   • Due to ↑ pressure in non-compliant LV

2. {hl}ANGINA PECTORIS:{/hl}
   • Due to ↑ O₂ demand by hypertrophied LV
   • Supply-demand mismatch (even without CAD)

3. {hl}SYNCOPE{/hl} (Temporary loss of consciousness):
   • During exertion
   • Due to inadequate cardiac output + ↓ BP

{key}OTHER SYMPTOMS:{/key}
• Acute pulmonary edema (possible)
• Sudden death (possible)

{key}PULSE:{/key}
• {hl}Slow-rising (parvus et tardus){/hl}
• Rapid fall in descending phase
• Best examined at {hl}CAROTID ARTERY{/hl}
• Narrow pulse pressure

{key}PALPATION:{/key}
• Apex beat: Usually normal position
• Sustained or double impulse (S4 or atrial kick)
• {hl}SYSTOLIC THRILL:{/hl} Palpable murmur at aortic area

{key}AUSCULTATION - MURMUR:{/key}
• Best heard at {hl}AORTIC AREA{/hl}
• Radiates to {hl}CAROTID ARTERIES{/hl}
• {hl}Harsh, crescendo-decrescendo{/hl} (saw-like)
• "Mew" sound in elderly
• Duration: Longer in severe obstruction
• {warn}INTENSITY does NOT correlate with severity{/warn}
• Very severe AS: Murmur may be {hlpu}INAUDIBLE{/hlpu}

{key}OTHER AUSCULTATORY FINDINGS:{/key}
• Systolic ejection click (if valve not calcified)
• Soft or inaudible S2 (if valve immobile/calcified)
• Reversed splitting of S2 (aortic component during expiration)
• Prominent S4

{key}SPECIAL CONSIDERATIONS IN ELDERLY (≥60 years):{/key}
• 20% have hypertension (50% moderate-severe)
• ↑ Arterial stiffness → Normal or rapid upstroke
• Pressure pulse may be wide
• Difficult to differentiate mild AS from aortic sclerosis (sclerosis = thickened but NOT stenotic)

{key}EARLY:{/key} May be {ok}NORMAL{/ok}

{key}LATE (PA view):{/key}
• {hl}POST-STENOTIC DILATATION{/hl} of ascending aorta
• Left ventricular enlargement

{key}LATERAL VIEW:{/key}
• {hl}Calcified aortic valve{/hl} (clearly visible)

{key}WITH HEART FAILURE:{/key}
• ↑ Cardio-thoracic ratio

{ok}AS = Obstruction to LEFT VENTRICULAR OUTFLOW during systole{/ok}

{ok}LOCATIONS: Valvular (most common), Supravalvular, Subvalvular{/ok}

{ok}CAUSES BY AGE:{/ok}
• Young: Congenital
• Young adults: Rheumatic + Bicuspid calcification
• Elderly: Calcific degeneration

{ok}Most common: Calcific degeneration of BICUSPID valve{/ok}

{ok}RISK FACTORS: Age, Male, ↑ LDL/Lp(a), HTN, DM, Smoking{/ok}

{ok}PATHOPHYSIOLOGY:{/ok}
• Pressure overload → Concentric LVH
• Relative ischemia → Angina (even without CAD)
• When valve area ↓ to 1/3 normal → Symptoms appear
• Without surgery: Death in 2-3 years after symptoms

{ok}CLASSIC TRIAD (Severe AS):{/ok}
1. Exertional Dyspnea
2. Angina Pectoris
3. Syncope (on exertion)

{ok}PULSE: Pulsus parvus et tardus (Slow-rising, low volume){/ok}
• Best examined at CAROTID ARTERY

{ok}MURMUR:{/ok}
• Harsh, crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur
• Best at AORTIC AREA, radiates to CAROTIDS
• Intensity does NOT correlate with severity
• May be INAUDIBLE in very severe AS

{ok}OTHER SIGNS:{/ok}
• Systolic ejection click (non-calcified valve)
• Soft/inaudible S2 (calcified valve)
• Reversed S2 splitting
• Prominent S4

{ok}CXR:{/ok}
• Post-stenotic dilatation of ascending aorta
• LV enlargement
• Calcified valve (lateral view)
• ↑ Cardio-thoracic ratio (with HF)
